+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The repository currently checks availability in several cart paths and updates inventory through product/availability services, while checkout and order status transitions are separate flows. The quantity shown by the v2 product mapper is the contractually authoritative stock value.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Centralize stock lookup, validation, decrement, and restoration behind a transactional service keyed by store and SKU/product identity.
+- Validate the complete prospective cart before persisting any mutation.
+- Make checkout and cancellation atomic and idempotent.
+- Map stock-derived storefront flags without overriding merchant availability.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing endpoint URLs, pricing, payment behavior, or inventory data model semantics beyond using the existing quantity field.
+- Introducing reservations or long-lived stock holds before checkout.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Use the existing product/availability quantity represented by the v2 product response as the stock source; avoid maintaining a second counter that could drift.
+- Put all multi-product validation and mutation in one transaction, with a validation pass before writes. This preserves all-or-nothing behavior for checkout and multi-update; per-line ad hoc writes were rejected because they permit partial state.
+- Key mutations by the actual product associated with each cart/order line, not list position or a mutable request index, preventing another product from being decremented.
+- Track cancellation through the persisted order status transition: only an active-to-CANCELED transition restores stock, while CANCELED-to-CANCELED is a no-op and any reverse transition is rejected.
+- Return a domain conflict exception mapped to HTTP 409 for stock and illegal status transitions, preserving existing success responses.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Concurrent checkouts] Two requests may observe the same stock → use transactional locking or an atomic conditional update and fail the transaction when affected rows do not match.
+- [Legacy orders] Existing canceled orders may not have a separate restoration marker → derive idempotency from status history/transition and ensure only the first persisted transition performs restoration.
+- [Variants and multiple availability rows] Stock lookup must use the same product/SKU and availability selection rules as the v2 mapper → reuse the existing inventory service rather than duplicating selection logic.
